      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2015 The Obesity Society.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "Circulating and adipose tissue markers of iron overload are increased in subjects with obesity. The aim is to study iron signals in adipose tissue."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Adipose tissue R2* values and hepatic iron concentration (HIC) were evaluated using magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) in 23 middle-aged subjects with obesity and 20 subjects without obesity."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Subcutaneous (SAT) and visceral adipose tissue (VAT) R2* were increased in subjects with obesity (P\u2009=\u20090.004 and P\u2009=\u20090.008) and correlated significantly and positively with HIC in all subjects. Strikingly, most of the associations of liver iron with metabolic parameters were replicated with SAT and VAT R2*. BMI, waist circumference, fat mass, HOMA value, and C-reactive protein positively correlated with HIC and SAT and VAT R2*. BMI or percent fat mass (but not insulin resistance) contributed independently to 26.8-34.8% of the variance in sex- and age-adjusted SAT or VAT R2* (β\u2009>\u20090.40, P\u2009<\u20090.005). Within subjects with obesity, total cholesterol independently contributed to 14.8% of sex- and age-adjusted VAT iron variance (β\u2009=\u20090.50, P\u2009=\u20090.025)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Increased R2* in adipose tissue, which might indicate iron content, runs in parallel to liver iron stores of subjects with obesity. VAT iron seems also associated with serum cholesterol within subjects with obesity."
          }
        ]
      },
